CC   IMGT/LIGM-DB annotation level: by annotators
CC   
CC   [2]  fragments.
CC   The J-genes presented here account for all but two (EU and DI) of
CC   the known human heavy chain amino acid sequences [1]. By sequence
CC   homology comparison the authors [1] postulate that J1 duplicated
CC   after human/mouse divergence creating the human J1 and J2 genes,
CC   and that the J3 gene duplicated to produce J4 and J5. Although the
CC   three pseudogenes are substantially different from the active
CC   genes, they have each preserved the 5' putative recombination
CC   recognition sequence, suggesting that they may undergo V-D-J
CC   recombination. In addition to the J- and D-regions homologous to
CC   the mouse locus, there are two other regions of homology 5' to
CC   DHQ52 in human and DQ52 in mouse [1]. The first is 'at'-rich and
CC   the second is 'gc'-rich (bases 333-357 and 424-451). They are
CC   separated by a 60 bp spacer in both mouse and human. [1] also
CC   reports two recombinant genes involving this region. [2] presents
CC   active V and epsilon-1 genes. The J-region in [2] was used as a
CC   probe for locating the epsilon-1 gene.
CC   The authors [1] have determined a physical map of this multigene
CC   region which contains (in this order) the J-region genes, the
CC   mu/alpha switch region, the four mu constant region exons, the mu
CC   membrane anchor peptide, and the delta constant region. Flanagan
CC   and Rabbitts (Nature 300, 709-713 (1982)) have determined the order
CC   of this region relative to two gamma-epsilon-alpha regions.
